# journey-service — copy to .env and adjust for your environment
#
# Docker image is built from the shared root Dockerfile:
#   docker build -f Dockerfile \
#     --build-arg SERVICE=journey-service \
#     --build-arg ENTRY=dist/index.js \
#     --build-arg PORT=3045 \
#     -t civitasone/journey-service:latest .
PORT=3045
HOST=0.0.0.0
DATABASE_URL=postgres://journey_svc:journey_dev_pw@localhost:5435/civitas_journey
DATABASE_POOL_SIZE=20
QUEUE_DRIVER=memory
CACHE_DRIVER=memory
CACHE_TTL=60

# ── Wait-step sweeper: cross-tenant scanner (BYPASSRLS) — REQUIRED IN PRODUCTION
#
# journey.* tables are FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY and the service connects as the
# least-privilege journey_svc role (NOBYPASSRLS). The wait sweeper
# (src/modules/steps/sweeper.ts) has no tenant context of its own — it must scan
# ALL tenants for `wait` steps whose resume_at has elapsed. A bare cross-tenant
# SELECT as journey_svc returns ZERO rows, so leaving this unset in production
# means the sweeper silently finds nothing and every parked `wait` step stays
# parked forever: any journey containing a wait never advances past it.
#
# It reads through the dedicated `journey_scanner` BYPASSRLS role (created by
# migration 0006_journey_scanner_role.sql). The scanner is READ-ONLY; the sweeper
# performs no writes at all. Each resume is republished as a per-tenant command
# and applied on DATABASE_URL inside the tenant-scoped transaction, so RLS
# re-checks every mutation.
#
# Leave this UNSET in dev. It falls back to DATABASE_URL, which is correct there
# because the service connects as the RLS-inert superuser — and the migration
# generates a RANDOM password for journey_scanner unless you supply one, so any
# fixed dev DSN written here would simply fail to authenticate.
#
# In production, inject the journey_scanner DSN from the secret manager, having
# set the password at migration time via the civitas.journey_scanner_password
# GUC. Rotate out-of-band with:
#   ALTER ROLE journey_scanner PASSWORD '<from-secrets>';
# JOURNEY_SCANNER_DATABASE_URL=postgres://journey_scanner:<from-secrets>@<db-host>:5432/civitas_journey

# ── `api_call` step egress allowlist
#
# An `api_call` step's URL is authored by a tenant, so without an allowlist the
# worker becomes an SSRF proxy into the cluster's internal network and the cloud
# metadata endpoint. Dispatch therefore FAILS CLOSED: with this unset, every
# api_call step fails terminally with API_CALL_NOT_CONFIGURED and the other three
# step types are unaffected.
#
# Comma-separated hostnames. An entry beginning with a dot matches that domain
# and its subdomains; anything else must match the host exactly. Loopback,
# link-local and RFC1918 literals are refused even if allowlisted.
# JOURNEY_API_CALL_ALLOWED_HOSTS=.partner.example.gov.in,hooks.example.com

# Permit plain http for api_call targets. Dev/test only — https is required
# otherwise, since the request carries an Idempotency-Key and tenant headers.
# JOURNEY_API_CALL_ALLOW_INSECURE=true

# Keycloak JWT (production)
KEYCLOAK_URL=http://civitasone-keycloak:8080
KEYCLOAK_REALM=civitasone
JWT_ALGORITHM=RS256

# Tests only — for local vitest without Keycloak, set JWT_ALGORITHM=HS256 and
# supply your own JWT_SECRET of at least 32 characters. No literal is written
# here: secret scanning treats a committed example value as a finding, and a
# shared "example" HS256 secret has a habit of reaching a real deployment.
# JWT_ALGORITHM=HS256
